.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd September 4, 2026
.Dt LOCALNETWORK 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm localnetwork
.Nd capability-mode kernel NetworkCmp provider
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the initial base-system provider for
.Dq system.Network
version
.Dq 1.0.0 .
It is installed in
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Network.cap
and launched only by
.Xr serviced 8 .
.Pp
The base provider is a connection broker.
It implements bounded DNS and TCP/UDP
.Em connect
operations directly on the kernel network stack from within capability mode,
and returns the resulting connected socket to the consumer rather than proxying
its data.
Each consumer receives a private capability-mode worker enlisted in its
coalition.
The worker cannot fork, execute, or receive unrelated descriptors.
Name resolution runs in-process: the worker resolves names itself, reading
.Pa /etc/resolv.conf ,
.Pa /etc/hosts ,
and
.Pa /etc/services
on demand through the filesystem provider.
No Casper channel is involved and nothing resolution-related crosses into the
consumer.
.Pp
On a
.Em connect
or
.Em udp
request the worker creates a socket, connects it under the session policy, and
sends the connected descriptor back over the session channel with
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS .
Before delivery the descriptor is limited with
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2
to only the data-path rights
.Pq read, write, event, shutdown, get/set socket option, fcntl, fstat, and ioctl ,
with
.Xr cap_ioctls_limit 2
restricting ioctls to
.Dv FIONREAD , FIONBIO ,
and
.Dv FIOASYNC ,
.Xr cap_fcntls_limit 2
restricting fcntl to
.Dv F_GETFL
and
.Dv F_SETFL ,
and its transfer state latched so it can be delivered exactly once.
The delivered descriptor cannot bind, connect, listen, accept, or peel off.
The consumer owns all subsequent I/O on it.
The provider retains no socket state after delivery.
.Pp
A
.Em connect
request may carry a
.Va timeout_ms
bound on the connect attempt.
A value of zero, the default, keeps the historical fully-blocking
.Xr connect 2 ;
a nonzero value bounds the wait, and a connect that has not completed when the
deadline expires fails with
.Er ETIMEDOUT .
The bound applies to TCP
.Em connect
only; a
.Em udp
request ignores it.
The version 1 protocol exposes only DNS, connect, and udp; protocol space for
future userspace networking is reserved but implemented as none, and any such
behavior requires a separately negotiated protocol version.
.Pp
Resolver requests cap host and service lengths, flags, address families, and
result counts.
Among the accepted resolver flags,
.Dv AI_V4MAPPED
and
.Dv AI_ALL
follow RFC 3493 for an
.Dv AF_INET6
query: when no native
.Sy AAAA
record is found, or when
.Dv AI_ALL
is also set,
.Sy A
records are fetched and appended as IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses.
.Dv AI_ADDRCONFIG
is accepted but is a deliberate no-op: the in-process resolver does not
enumerate the host's own configured source families, so it never suppresses a
family on that basis.
It never reads resolver configuration from the consumer filesystem.
Resolution runs on a dedicated thread that issues its own bounded DNS queries,
so a slow resolver cannot stop socket operations on the session.
At most one resolution is active per private session; an overlapping request
fails with
.Er EBUSY .
If a client abandons or times out a request, the channel reply token prevents
the eventual late result from being mistaken for another reply.
The provider also enforces a 30-second resolver deadline.
Because the resolver interface does not provide safe in-thread cancellation,
exceeding that deadline terminates only the consumer's private NetworkCmp
worker; the typed client observes peer death and may recover according to its
component lifecycle policy.
This fail-closed boundary prevents a stuck resolver from retaining resources
or making every later resolve return
.Er EBUSY
indefinitely.
.Pp
The component bootstrap carries no client-selected policy object.
The session policy is resolved once at session creation and is immutable
thereafter; the client never supplies policy on a request.
A session whose granted channel carries the administrative right receives the
full policy, including internal destinations; every other session is scoped by
the provider's per-client policy configuration described below, keyed by the
unforgeable serviced manifest label.
Listener authority is not part of the version 1 client API.
Socket options are not restricted by the broker: the consumer sets them itself
on the delivered descriptor within the delegated
.Dv CAP_SETSOCKOPT
right.
.Sh POLICY CONFIGURATION
Per-client policy is read once at startup, before capability mode, from
.Pa Config/localnetwork.conf
inside the provider's unit directory
.Pq Ev CAPABILITY_UNIT_DIR ,
that is
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Network.cap/Units/localnetwork.unit/Config/localnetwork.conf .
The file is UCL:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
default {
	resolve = true;
	connect = true;
	udp = true;
	inet4 = true;
	inet6 = true;
	internal = false;
}
clients {
	"org.example.render" { connect = false; udp = false; }
	"org.example.metrics" { internal = true; }
}
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Ic default
block applies to any client label without a
.Ic clients
entry; a
.Ic clients
entry names only the dimensions it overrides and inherits the rest from
.Ic default .
Every dimension is boolean:
.Ic resolve
(DNS),
.Ic connect
(TCP),
.Ic udp
(connected datagram),
.Ic inet4
and
.Ic inet6
(address families), and
.Ic internal
(loopback, link-local, and RFC1918/ULA destinations, denied by default; this
is the SSRF guard).
A label whose resolved policy permits no operation is refused a session with
.Er EACCES .
Sessions whose channel carries the administrative right bypass this table
entirely.
.Pp
The shipped default equals the compiled-in policy: outbound resolve, TCP, and
UDP over both address families permitted, internal destinations denied, and at
most 16 resolver results per session.
The entry schema is closed \(em an unknown key, a non-boolean value, or an
invalid or duplicate label makes the file malformed \(em but the file-level
failure direction is fail-soft: a missing or malformed file leaves the
compiled-in default policy in force and logs a warning, so a bad
configuration can never take the network provider down with it.
The applied policy source
.Pq admin, label, or default
is logged at each session start.
.Sh SECURITY
Session creation and resolver policy decisions are recorded through OpenBSM.
Session, resolution, connect, and rejection paths expose DTrace probes through
the
.Dq localnetwork_provider
provider.
The
.Sy connect-done
probe reports the client label, the
.Dq addr:port
dialed, the requested timeout in milliseconds
.Pq zero for a blocking connect ,
and the errno-style outcome
.Pq zero connected, Er ETIMEDOUT No on expiry .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libnetworkcmp 3 ,
.Xr serviced 8
